\f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\fs24 Information-Security Principles
\par }{
\par }{\i From the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services Web site
\par }{
\par }{\cgrid0 Principle 1: Information assets must be protected from loss, corruption, or disclosure.  Protection measures must be balanced against the operation cost imposed on the business, and must be appropriate to the threat and impact those assets face.

\par 
\par Principle 2: As threats evolve, the security posture must be re-evaluated and protections adjusted appropriately.
\par 
\par Principle 3: The information owner determines the degree of acceptable risk; the level of protection must be appropriate to the risk and the value of the asset being protected.
\par 
\par Principle 4: Protective measures must not prevent necessary business from being carried out.
\par 
\par Principle 5: Security protections must be implemented in a layered, mutually reinforcing way. This can be accomplished by selecting an appropriate combination of technical controls; physical controls; administrative controls; training and a
wareness efforts; and auditing, logging, and
\par incident-response to mediate risk and minimize impact from security events.
\par 
\par Principle 6: Security controls need to be designed as products or processes integrated from the beginning of the software/systems deve
lopment, rather than added as an afterthought. Security is but another aspect of quality. A system should do only what it is designed to do\emdash no more, no less.  Poor quality is a security risk.
\par 
\par Principle 7: Security mechanisms should balance the level of as
surance with ease-of-use. The user should not be overly encumbered by security to the extent that productivity is seriously impacted or a strong motivation to work around security safeguards is generated.
\par 
\par Principle 8: The organization\rquote s information policies and implementation shall fully comply with all Federal laws and regulations.
\par }{
